  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Friday)\nIssued at 112 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nA slight chance of showers/storms exists into Saturday for parts\nof far eastern Oklahoma and northwest Arkansas on the eastern\nperiphery of an expanding ridge of high pressure into the region.\nThe combination of moisture return today and the building high \npressure over the southern Plains Saturday will create hot and \nhumid conditions expanding over the CWA Saturday afternoon. High \ntemperatures in the 90s to near 100 degrees are forecast, along \nwith heat index values of 100 to 115 degrees. Thus, an Extreme\nHeat Warning has been issued for most of northeast Oklahoma, and\na Heat Advisory issued for the rest of eastern Oklahoma and also \nmuch of northwest Arkansas. At this time, have left \nWashington/Madison counties out of the advisory, though there \ncould be a brief window where heat index values near 105 degrees \ncould develop. \n\nHigh pressure ridge becomes the dominate feature over the region\nfor the weekend and into the first half of next week. Dangerous\nheat and humidity remain common through this period as the eastern\nperiphery of the low level thermal ridge expands into eastern\nOklahoma. These conditions are anticipated through Tuesday before\nthe ridge tries to retreat westward. This would put the CWA on the\neastern periphery of the ridge, and allow for a wave and\nassociated weak frontal boundary to sag into the region during the\nsecond half of next week. Depending on where the boundary sets up,\nparts of the CWA could see a slight break in the heat/humidity,\nwhile other parts could see isolated diurnal storm development\neach day. Details still remain uncertain but this is something to\nwatch, while the CWA goes through high temperatures in the upper\n90s to near 104 degrees Sunday - Wednesday.",
